E7 gate - the teeth (increment 22). 2026-11-01.
image:    sls_operating_system_e3_teeth.iso - make x86-iso-e3 E3_INIT_FEATURES=e3_envs,e7_gate
          E7_TOOTH=1 E7_SLOW_BOOT=0 (the only build carrying the three arms)
machine:  qemu-system-x86_64 -m 1G -smp 2 -accel tcg,thread=multi, 10 GiB NVMe
invocation: tests/e7_gate_check.sh --teeth  (E7G_BUILD=0 reuses the image above)
one capture (boot 1) then FOUR replays, each over its OWN sparse copy of the
post-checkpoint image: control (no tooth), descriptor, no-fd, no-heap. Boot 2
mutates the image it runs on, so a replay that has to be repeated gets a fresh
copy; this invocation needed no repeat (`note:` lines would say so, and a pour
that refuses every attempt fails the run rather than being retried away).
Each recording is also --replay-able on its own (facts carries tooth=<name>,
and the harness validates it under that expectation). The `--- key lines` under
each recording are the transcript lines its clauses read, in the order they
appear in the serial transcript (kernel and tenant share one line stream, so a
merged line is two writers and not a reordering); the full boot2.log of each
replay stays in the run's artifact directory.

ok:   G1. the drill is a build-time feature (sidecar Cargo + Makefile knob) whose stretch runs in the gate profile between staging (line 602) and the tenant spawn (line 620)
ok:   G2. the wait is bounded by EP_WAIT_TICKS (stall, restarted by progress) and EP_WAIT_CEIL_TICKS (absolute), and its progress signal is ProcessDescriptor.switches, bumped at proc_build_resume_frame
ok:   G3. the wait reports crossing the old fixed bound once, only while the window is still open (progress restarted it), and names how many of the record's sidecars are RUNNING at that instant
ok:   G4. the gate tenant is the designated 9-entry command line over the 512 KiB staged image, and the checkpoint's stop is the copy continuation at 196608
ok:   G5. the restore pass waits before the repause and pours after it, and the route reports payloads and payload_skips by name
ok:   G6. the teeth are a build-time profile (E7_TOOTH -> AB_DEFS, default off) selected per restore and refused by name elsewhere; all three arms land on the shipped refusals (dropped record, EBADF lookup, unmapped destination), guarded by 11 E7G_TOOTH conditions

